.TH CVEC_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_destroy \- destroy a vector and clear the handle (type-inferred wrapper)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_destroy(v);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro frees the backing buffer of the vector and the container itself.
It then sets v to NULL. It leaves a handle that is NULL already alone, and
it frees nothing for such a handle. A second cvec_destroy() of the same
variable therefore does nothing and is safe.

The macro frees everything through the memory management procedures that
created the vector. It frees the private copy of that procedure table in
the same way. This macro therefore needs no allocator argument. This is the
name that the type-inferred macro layer uses. The macro is cvector_destroy(3)
under another name, and it behaves in the same way.

The macro clears the handle and nothing else. cvec_declare(3) and the
construct macros declare the companion type variables. Those variables stay
in scope and stay valid. cvec_init(3) or cvec_init_mp(3) can therefore give
the same variable a new vector later.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v  A cvec lvalue that names the vector to destroy, such as a variable or
   an element of an array. The macro must be able to assign to it. The
   macro evaluates it exactly once, so an expression with a side effect,
   such as vs[i++], destroys and clears one element. The macro sets it to
   NULL before it returns. An lvalue that holds NULL makes the macro do
   nothing.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to a statement, not to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro does not free the elements one by one when the vector holds
pointers to heap buffers. The caller must free the element data first.
This call invalidates every pointer from an earlier cvec_data_ptr(3) or
cvec_at_ptr(3).
cvec_declare_scoped(3), cvec_construct_scoped(3) and
cvec_construct_mp_scoped(3) destroy their vector at the end of the scope
already. They also clear the handle as they do this. An explicit call
inside such a block is legal, but it is not necessary.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Destroy a vector one time, through one handle. A cvec that you copy into
a second variable leaves that copy dangling. A destruction through that
copy afterwards is a double free.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_push(vec, 42);

cvec_destroy(vec);   /* vec is NULL after this call */
cvec_destroy(vec);   /* this call does nothing and is safe */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_destroy (3),
.BR cvec_construct (3),
.BR cvec_init (3),
.BR cvec_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_reset (3)
